{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>לְמַעַן טַמֵּא אֶת מִקְדָּשִׁי — to defile My sanctuary</b>. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 20:3): \"the congregation of Israel, which is sanctified to Me.\" I have already hinted at this (Ramban on Leviticus 18:21) — how does a single man who sins defile the congregation of Israel, which is sanctified to His great Name? They have already alluded to this in their midrash, as they said (Berakhot 35a): anyone who enjoys this world without a blessing is as though he robs the Holy One, blessed be He, and the congregation of Israel, as it is written (Proverbs 28:24), \"He who robs his father and his mother,\" etc. — \"his father\" is none other than the Holy One, blessed be He, and \"his mother\" is none other than the congregation of Israel; \"he is the companion of a destructive man\" — he is the companion of Jeroboam son of Nebat, who caused Israel to sin against their Father in heaven. For the purpose of creation is that they bless His great Name upon it, and from there comes the sustenance of the world; and if His great Name is not blessed, the Shekhinah departs from Israel — all the more so when one offers the fruit to Molech, who abhors the pride of Jacob and His dwelling. And this is the reason for \"the people of the land shall stone him\" (Leviticus 20:2) — for it does not say \"he shall surely be put to death, they shall stone him,\" as it says in all the other cases, but rather it mentions \"the people of the land,\" to say that all the people of the land, all Israel, are obligated to take the initiative and stone him, for he causes harm to all of them, for he causes the Shekhinah to depart from Israel. Therefore Onkelos translated \"the people of the house of Israel,\" for \"the land\" alludes to the entire land of Israel, not to the land where the worshipper dwells, as in the manner of \"for the land is Mine\" (Leviticus 25:23). And further, you will be able to understand its mystery from the fact that Scripture mentions in the excision of Molech, \"I will set My face against that man\" (here), and likewise, \"and I Myself will set My face\" (Leviticus 20:5) — for His great and awesome Name will cut him off. And it did not mention the word \"I\" in the excision of the ov and the yidde'oni, nor in any other excision. I have already made known this reason to every discerning person."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "Ramban writes לְהַיְצִירָה in some editions and בַּיְּצִירָה in others; I read it as 'in the act of creation' following the context, which speaks of the purpose of creation and blessings recited upon it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:56:54.050485951Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}